ory degree yet you become a famous  historian right  that is also a thing that happened  hero of two worlds chronicles  lafayette's life as a kind of forest  gump in the age of revolution a man who  always found himself in the middle of  epic events  marie joseph paul eve roche gilbert du  matiere marquita lafayette was born in a  french nobility in 1757 from an early  age it was clear he was not happy living  the stuffy life of an aristocrat his  first bold move was traveling to the u.s  when he was just a teenager to help the  americans fight the revolution he defies  his father-in-law he defies the king of  france he defies the the the considered  opinion of the aristocracy he buys his  own boat he sails to the united states  and he joins the continental army  lafayette was declared a general in the  continental army before turning 20.  early on he was wounded in the battle of  brandywine and while the americans lost  that round lafayette's bravery earned  him the notice of washington it was at  washington's side that he also weathered  a famously harsh winter at valley forge  and helped capture the british commander  lord cornwallis dealing a decisive blow  to the redcoats if lafayette had not  been part of the revolution would  americans have won the revolution we  know for sure that the united states  does not become a thing without french  assistance um the continental army is  not going to be able to beat the british  army and the british navy without the  help of the french lafayette was at the  very leading edge of official  recognition of the united states by  france and the movement by the kingdom  of france to support the colonies so  absent lafayette does france get into  the war  maybe yes but with lafayette there  absolutely  after helping the continental army win  and spending two long stretches in the  new world lafayette went home and in  1789 found himself in the middle of  another revolution  he supported the french people in their  quest for democracy  but unlike with the americans didn't  feel they were ready to fully abandon  their monarchy those split loyalties  came at a cost lafayette's great asset  was his perseverance his ability to  endure and continue to carry forward i  mean he almost gets his head chopped off  in the french revolution that doesn't  make him stop he winds up in a dungeon  for five years that doesn't make him  stop in 1824 lafayette returned to the  u.s visiting every state in the young  country while getting an official place  in the pantheon of heroes of american  independence there are hundreds if not  thousands of parks and cities and towns  across the country named fayette  lafayette fayetteville yeah there are  probably thousands at this point um and  this park was named after lafayette when  he was here in over 1824 in 1825. a  statue in the park facing the white  house was added in 1891. if he came back  and saw us today yeah he'd be very happy  he'd be very happy himself yeah and and  he would i'm sure give a suitably humble  speech about how it wasn't him it was  everybody together and he's just you  know he's just here and he loves every  minute of this  as debates around the country continue  about monuments to our nation's earliest  leaders lafayette's legacy and  reputation have remained largely intact  not just because you always strive for  the same ideals people still want today  like unity and equality  but also his position on slavery after  the war and after he goes back to france  he decides  that liberty and slavery are in fact  incompatible and that if the american  revolution is going to fulfill itself uh  and make the mark on the world and on  history that it needs to make  the americas need to free their slaves  and he  begins writing letters to his friend his  father figure george washington saying  you should set an example for your  countrymen and you should free your  slaves and prove to the world that  emancipation will not be the economic  ruin of 